// ora-mod-sla — generated by tools/merge.js: src/ora-mod-sla-*.js plus matching questions from the Oracle role banks (tools/modules.js).
// Oracle Modules — SLA, Tax & Accounting Hub. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D']
QB['ora-mod-sla'] = (QB['ora-mod-sla'] || []).concat([
['SLA', 'Explain the SLA hierarchy: accounting method, rule sets and rules.',
`- **Subledger accounting method** (e.g. Standard Accrual) assigned to a ledger — groups the rules for all subledgers.
- **Journal entry rule sets** per **event class/type** (e.g. Payables Invoices) — attached to the method.
- Inside rule sets: **journal line rules** (debit/credit, accounting class, conditions), **account derivation rules** (how to build accounts), **description rules**, **supporting references**.
- **Mapping sets** and **sources** feed the rules.
- After changes: **activate** the rule set (Fusion validates and compiles).`, 'D'],

['SLA', 'What is the difference between "Create Accounting" draft, final and final post?',
`- **Draft**: entries created for review; can be deleted and recreated; not transferred to GL.
- **Final**: locked entries; transferred to GL (if transfer selected).
- **Final post**: transferred and posted in GL in one run.
- Use draft to test rule changes; final for production runs.`, 'B'],

['SLA', 'How do account derivation rules build an account?',
`- Rule types: **account combination** (whole account), **segment** (one segment) or **value set**-based.
- Conditions choose which rule applies (e.g. by supplier type, item category).
- Values come from **constants**, **sources** (transaction attributes, DFFs) or **mapping sets**.
- Combine: start from a default account (e.g. from the distribution) and override specific segments (natural account from mapping, cost center from the transaction).
- Order/priority matters — first matching condition wins.`, 'D'],

['SLA', 'How do you add a custom source to SLA rules?',
`- Use a **descriptive flexfield** on the transaction (e.g. invoice line) exposed as a source, or create a **custom source** (where supported) based on existing sources/formulas.
- Assign it to the event class; use it in conditions or derivation rules.
- Test with transactions that populate and don't populate the field.`, 'D'],

['SLA', 'What are accounting classes and why do they matter?',
`- Each journal line gets an **accounting class** (Liability, Item Expense, Receivable, Revenue, Gain, Loss, Cash Clearing…).
- Used for **reporting**, **third-party balances**, **reconciliation reports** and rule conditions (e.g. override only the Liability class).
- Mention you use them to validate entries in testing.`, 'B'],

['SLA', 'How do you correct accounting that was wrong after Create Accounting in final mode?',
`- You can't edit final SLA entries. Options:
- **Fix the source transaction** (e.g. adjust distribution) — creates new events and accounting.
- **Manual subledger journal entries** (SLA manual entries) with the same audit trail.
- **GL journal** as last resort (breaks subledger-to-GL reconciliation unless reclassed with care).
- Fix the **rule** for future transactions.`, 'D'],

['SLA', 'What is the purpose of supporting references and third-party balances?',
`- **Supporting references** store extra attributes on journal lines and keep **balances by those values** (e.g. by contract, project, supplier) — useful for reconciliation and analysis.
- **Third-party balances**: balances by supplier/customer on control accounts — supports supplier/customer balance reports and reconciliation.`, 'D'],

['SLA', 'What diagnostics help with SLA issues?',
`- **Create Accounting execution report** (errors per event).
- **Subledger journal entry** inquiry with **view accounting** and **account derivation** details.
- **Accounting event diagnostics** (enable SLA diagnostics profile, run diagnostics report) — shows source values used.
- **Unaccounted transactions** reports by subledger.`, 'D'],

['Tax', 'Explain the Fusion Tax configuration hierarchy.',
`- **Tax regime** (country/tax type, e.g. US Sales Tax, UK VAT) → **Tax** → **Tax status** (standard, reduced, exempt) → **Tax rate** (with effective dates) → **Tax jurisdiction** (geography).
- **Tax rules**: determine place of supply, applicability, status, rate, taxable basis, calculation, recovery.
- **Party tax profiles** and **registrations** for legal entities, suppliers, customers.
- **Product tax classification** for items/services.
- **Configuration owner** controls (global vs per legal entity).`, 'D'],

['Tax', 'How do you handle VAT recovery on purchases?',
`- **Recovery rates** (100%, partial, 0%) on tax rates, with rules (e.g. non-recoverable for entertainment).
- Recoverable tax → **tax receivable/input VAT** account; non-recoverable → expense or asset cost.
- **Partial recovery** for mixed-use businesses.
- Reports for VAT returns from tax reporting ledger.`, 'D'],

['Tax', 'What is the tax reporting ledger and how is it used?',
`- Stores tax lines from AP/AR/GL (and other sources) with attributes needed for **tax returns** (VAT returns, sales/purchase registers, EC sales lists).
- Run **Select Tax Reporting Data**/extracts and country-specific reports.
- Supports **reconciliation** of tax accounts to tax reports.`, 'D'],

['Tax', 'How do withholding taxes work in Fusion Payables?',
`- **Withholding tax regime/codes** and rates; supplier site withholding applicability.
- At invoice validation or payment, Oracle withholds tax and creates a **withholding tax invoice** to the tax authority (supplier).
- Certificates and reporting by jurisdiction.
- Common in Latin America, India, some EU countries.`, 'D'],

['Tax', 'What are e-invoicing mandates and how do they affect Oracle?',
`- Many countries require **electronic invoices** cleared or reported to tax authorities (Mexico CFDI, Italy SDI, India IRN, Saudi ZATCA, PEPPOL-based regimes, and upcoming EU ViDA requirements).
- **Oracle approach**: localizations and partner integrations to generate/submit invoices, receive acknowledgments, and store legal documents.
- Plan per country with tax/legal teams; test early.`, 'D'],

['Accounting Hub', 'How do you implement Fusion Accounting Hub for a non-Oracle source?',
`- Register a **subledger application**; define the **event model** (entities, event classes/types).
- Define **sources** (transaction attributes) and **source assignments**.
- Build **accounting rules** (journal line rules, ADRs, mapping sets) and an **accounting method**.
- Load transactions via **FBDI/REST** (Accounting Hub transaction objects), run **Create Accounting**.
- Reconcile source → FAH → GL; drill down available for analysis.`, 'D'],

['Accounting Hub', 'Why use Accounting Hub instead of summarized GL journal imports?',
`- **Accounting rules centrally controlled** by finance (not hard-coded in source systems).
- **Multi-GAAP** from the same transactions (multiple methods/ledgers).
- **Detailed audit trail** and drill-down; supporting references for reporting.
- Better **reconciliation** with source systems.
- Trade-off: more design and data volume.`, 'D'],

['Interview', 'Give an example of an SLA rule change you made and how you tested it.',
`Shape:
- Business need (e.g. separate AP liability account for intercompany suppliers).
- Rule change (copied method, mapping set by supplier type, ADR on liability line).
- Tests: invoice, payment, cancellation, prepayment application, credit memo — draft accounting first, then final in test.
- Result and controls (documentation, approval, audit evidence).`, 'B']
]);

QB['ora-mod-sla'] = (QB['ora-mod-sla'] || []).concat([["Accounting Hub","What is Fusion Accounting Hub (FAH) and when would you use it?","FAH lets you create **subledger accounting from non-Oracle source systems** (banking, insurance policy admin, billing, loans) using the same **SLA rules engine**.\n- Define a **subledger application**, **event model** (event classes/types), **sources** (transaction attributes) and **accounting rules**.\n- Load transactions (FBDI/REST), run Create Accounting → journals to GL with drill-down to source attributes.\n- Supports **multi-GAAP** (e.g. IFRS 17, US GAAP) through multiple accounting methods.\n\nUse it when the source system can't produce good accounting itself, or rules must be controlled by finance in one place.","D"],["Tax","How would you architect indirect tax for a global Fusion implementation?","- **Fusion Tax** natively for VAT/GST countries (rules-based); **partner engine** (Vertex/Avalara/Sovos) for US sales/use tax and possibly global.\n- **E-invoicing mandates** (Italy SDI, Mexico CFDI, India e-invoice, PEPPOL, upcoming EU ViDA) via Oracle localizations or partners.\n- **Tax reporting** (VAT returns, SAF-T) from Fusion tax reporting ledger or partner.\n- **Master data**: party tax profiles, registrations, product tax classification.\n- Governance: tax team owns rules; changes through change control; regression tests for rate changes.","D"],["Subledger Accounting","How would you design SLA for complex revenue and cost accounting across multiple GAAPs?","- Inventory all **event classes** that need non-standard accounting.\n- **Copy** seeded methods once per GAAP (e.g. US GAAP method, IFRS method), maximize **reuse** of rules (shared ADRs, mapping sets).\n- **Secondary ledger** with the second method at subledger level.\n- Use **custom sources** sparingly — prefer seeded sources and DFFs mapped via the source mapping.\n- **Supporting references** for reconciliation (contract ID, product).\n- Document each rule with business rationale; test with an accounting matrix.\n- Governance: finance owns rules; IT owns deployment.","D"],["Subledger Accounting","What is Create Accounting and why does it matter?","Subledger transactions (invoices, receipts, assets) don't hit GL by themselves. **Create Accounting** runs Subledger Accounting to build journal entries from those events, then (optionally) **transfers and posts** them to GL.\n\n- Modes: **Draft** (preview, can change) or **Final** (locked).\n- Errors (e.g. invalid account) appear in the Create Accounting execution report — fix them and rerun.\n- Run it frequently (daily) to catch issues early, not just at month end.","B"],["Subledger Accounting","How do you find out why a transaction did not create accounting?","- Check the **Create Accounting execution report** for the error message.\n- View the transaction's accounting status (e.g. invoice \"Unaccounted\" vs \"Accounted\").\n- Common causes: transaction not validated/approved, period closed in the subledger, invalid or disabled account combination, cross-validation rule failure, missing mapping in an account derivation rule.\n- Use **Review Subledger Journal Entries** to see draft entries and errors.\n- Fix the root cause, then rerun Create Accounting for that transaction or batch.","D"],["Tax","How is tax calculated on transactions in Oracle Fusion?","Oracle uses **Tax (E-Business Tax / Fusion Tax)** with a hierarchy:\n- **Tax regime** (e.g. US Sales Tax, UK VAT) → **Tax** → **Tax status** → **Tax rate** → **Tax jurisdiction**.\n- **Tax rules** decide when a tax applies and which rate — based on parties, locations, product and transaction details.\n- Parties need **tax profiles** and registrations.\n- The tax is calculated on AP invoices, AR transactions and POs and shown on separate tax lines with their own accounts.\n- For US sales tax many companies integrate with a partner (Vertex, Avalara).","D"],["Subledger Accounting","Explain Subledger Accounting (SLA) and give an example of a rule you customized.","SLA is the engine that turns subledger events (an AP invoice validated, a receipt applied) into accounting entries before they go to GL. It separates \"what happened\" from \"how we account for it\", so the rules can change without touching transactions.\n\n## Key components\n- **Event classes and types** — what happened (e.g. Invoices → Invoice Validated).\n- **Journal line rules** — debit/credit, accounting class, conditions.\n- **Account derivation rules (ADRs)** — how each account segment is derived (from the transaction, a mapping set or a constant).\n- **Description rules** and **supporting references** for reporting and reconciliation.\n- **Subledger Accounting Method** assigned to the ledger, grouping the application accounting definitions.\n\n## Example\nOn one project the business wanted AP liability booked to a different natural account by supplier type (intercompany vs third-party). I copied the standard method, created an ADR that overrode only the natural-account segment using a mapping set driven by supplier type, attached it to the liability journal line rule, validated, and regression-tested invoice, payment and cancellation flows.\n\nTip: Always say you copy the seeded method rather than editing it, and that you test the full event lifecycle (create, adjust, cancel, pay).","D"],["Tax","How do you integrate a third-party tax engine like Vertex or Avalara?","- Fusion Tax provides **tax partner integration** — the partner calculates US sales/use tax.\n- Set up the **tax regime** for partner services, provider configuration, and **tax partner subscriptions** for the BUs/legal entities.\n- Item tax codes/product categories and **ship-from/ship-to** locations must be accurate (geocodes).\n- Transactions (AR invoices, AP invoices, POs) call the partner for calculation; results stored on tax lines.\n- Reporting/filing done in the partner tool; reconcile tax accounts in GL.\n- Test exemptions (exempt customers with certificates), use tax on AP, and jurisdiction edge cases.","D"],["Tax","How would you manage US sales tax exemptions in Fusion?","- Capture **exemption certificates** per customer and jurisdiction (in Fusion Tax or the partner engine, e.g. Vertex/Avalara).\n- Link exemptions to customer tax profiles; effective dates.\n- **Audit** expiring certificates; reminders to customers.\n- Test transactions with exempt and taxable lines.","D"],["Subledger Accounting","How would you change the liability account on AP invoices based on supplier type using SLA?","- Navigate to **Manage Accounting Methods**; **copy** the seeded Standard Accrual method (never edit seeded).\n- Create a **mapping set**: input = supplier type (supplier attribute source); output = natural account value.\n- Create an **account derivation rule** for the liability that takes the default account and **overrides the natural account segment** with the mapping set output.\n- Copy the seeded **journal entry rule set** for the Invoices event class and attach the new ADR to the **Liability** journal line.\n- **Activate** the rule set, assign the custom method to the ledger.\n- Test invoice, payment, cancellation and prepayment application.","D"],["Subledger Accounting","What are mapping sets, sources and supporting references in SLA?","- **Sources** — the transaction data SLA can use in rules (e.g. supplier type, item category, project). Custom sources can be built from attributes/DFFs.\n- **Mapping sets** — lookup tables that turn input values into a segment value or full account (e.g. category → natural account).\n- **Supporting references** — extra attributes stored with journal lines (e.g. supplier, invoice number) so you can report balances by them and reconcile.\n\nSupporting references are very useful for reconciliations like intercompany or tax accounts.","D"],["Tax","How would you set up a new tax rate, for example a VAT rate change in a country?","- In **Manage Tax Rates**, **end-date** the current rate and create a new rate with the effective date (or add a new rate period under the same rate code).\n- Update tax rules if the rate determination changes.\n- Check **tax recovery rates** and accounts.\n- Test transactions dated before and after the change (AP, AR, PO).\n- Communicate to AP/AR teams, and check reports and tax filings for the switch-over period.\n\nTip: Mention transactions that span the date (e.g. invoices dated before, paid after).","D"]]);
